        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>Does treadmill burn belly fat?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Not selectively — no exercise can target fat in one area. A treadmill helps you lose fat from your whole body, belly included, by adding to an energy deficit over time. Regular aerobic exercise has also been shown in controlled trials to reduce visceral fat, the deep abdominal fat most linked to health risk, sometimes even before the scale moves much.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Will a treadmill burn belly fat faster than other exercise?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              No exercise burns belly fat faster than any other, because the body decides where fat comes off. What matters is the total energy you use over weeks and months, and how consistently you keep it up. A treadmill's advantage is convenience: it removes weather and daylight as excuses, which makes a daily habit easier to sustain.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Does treadmill reduce belly fat if I only walk?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It can. Brisk walking, especially with some incline, is a sustainable way to add regular activity, and walking counts toward the same weekly activity guidelines as running. Combined with sensible eating over a period of months, walking is enough for many people to see their waist measurement come down. Expect the lower stomach to be among the last areas to change.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Does treadmill burn fat?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Yes, in the sense that treadmill exercise uses energy and, over time and alongside your eating habits, can contribute to fat loss across the body. The fat-burning zone on the console is less important than it looks: higher intensities burn more energy overall, and long-term energy balance matters more than which fuel you used during the session.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can incline on a treadmill help you lose thigh fat?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Not directly. Incline walking works the glutes, hamstrings and calves harder and raises the energy cost of walking considerably — a 10% grade roughly doubles it at a typical walking pace — which helps whole-body fat loss. But fat comes off the thighs on the same body-wide schedule as everywhere else, and thighs are often one of the last areas to change.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How long does it take to lose belly fat on a treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Usually months rather than weeks. Fitness improves within a few weeks, the waist measurement may start moving within the first couple of months, and visible change in stubborn areas comes last. Mainstream guidance treats 1 to 2 pounds a week as a sustainable pace for people with weight to lose, and slower is normal. Judge progress over four to eight weeks, not day to day.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

